// Rejects: a type parameter on a trait declaration
//
// `TraitDecl` has no field for one, so `parse_trait_decl` skipped it silently
// (#1164). The parameter name then resolved to nothing in the method
// signatures, and the bill arrived at the conformance:
//
//   error[E0333]: `Meters` does not implement `Scale<f64>`
//       | `Meters` is missing methods `Scale<f64>` requires
//       = fix: add the missing methods to the block
//
// pointing at a block with the method right there. The required signature said
// `k: Rhs`, `Rhs` named nothing, and the conformance's `k: f64` couldn't match
// it. Writing the header as bare `extend Meters with Scale` gave the same
// error, so it wasn't the `<f64>` — it was the trait side all along.
//
// Parser-level, so the checker never runs here. The unknown-type-in-a-trait-
// signature half is in trait_signature_unknown_type.rk.

// ERROR: a type parameter on a trait — generic traits aren't implemented
trait Scale<Rhs> {
    func scale(self, k: f64) -> Self
}

// What works today — name the type concretely:
//
//   trait Scale {
//       func scale(self, k: f64) -> Self
//   }
//
//   extend Meters with Scale {
//       func scale(self, k: f64) -> Meters {
//           return Meters { v: self.v * k }
//       }
//   }
//
// The generic form is what `trait Mul<Rhs>` needs for operator resolution, and
// it's blocked on #1165 along with associated types.
